WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.770
The ideal is quality and quantity,
but face it, you're only human.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:05.640
This is higher ed.

3
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:06.240
Hot takes.

4
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.105
And I'm Joel Goodman.

5
00:00:20.287 --> 00:00:22.327
Work smarter, not harder.

6
00:00:22.687 --> 00:00:25.447
This old adage has helped
many of us do our jobs better.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:28.357
If you're an oldie, maybe that
meant setting up macros in your

8
00:00:28.357 --> 00:00:31.777
word processor for actions that
you needed to repeat all the time.

9
00:00:32.137 --> 00:00:35.407
Maybe it meant setting up labels and
folders in your email inbox and using

10
00:00:35.407 --> 00:00:37.027
rules to sort incoming messages.

11
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:40.357
Or maybe it's using tools like
Zapier to automate different tasks.

12
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:44.497
Efficiency saves us a lot of time
In a bakery or factory, being

13
00:00:44.497 --> 00:00:47.017
efficient with motion can reduce
strain and toll on your body.

14
00:00:47.227 --> 00:00:50.497
In digital work, you can prevent carpal
tunnel or even feel a little more

15
00:00:50.497 --> 00:00:52.747
superhuman with some productivity hacks.

16
00:00:53.347 --> 00:00:56.827
Efficiency is good then, and it's
helped the world accomplish a lot.

17
00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.017
Episode over what?

18
00:00:59.077 --> 00:01:00.097
What was I talking about?

19
00:01:00.607 --> 00:01:05.797
Uh, so there's a dark side to creating
all this extra time Efficiency

20
00:01:05.797 --> 00:01:08.197
has an insatiable hunger for time.

21
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:12.937
Anytime saved has to be filled with
more production, and then you have to

22
00:01:12.937 --> 00:01:14.377
make that production more efficient.

23
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:19.447
It's a terrible cycle and it's
bad, just really, really bad.

24
00:01:19.927 --> 00:01:23.257
In higher ed, this shows its
face as doing more with less.

25
00:01:23.722 --> 00:01:25.702
Uh, you've heard it, I've heard it.

26
00:01:26.092 --> 00:01:28.882
If we're honest, you're probably
living it three or four times

27
00:01:28.882 --> 00:01:32.602
over at this point, and the only
answer is to be more efficient.

28
00:01:33.022 --> 00:01:36.772
The problem is that you've exhausted
all of the productivity hacks and

29
00:01:36.772 --> 00:01:39.262
don't have any more time outside
of what are supposed to be normal

30
00:01:39.262 --> 00:01:40.972
work hours to get more stuff done.

31
00:01:41.572 --> 00:01:44.722
In 2025, the answer is ai.

32
00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:47.542
I'm sorry.

33
00:01:48.112 --> 00:01:49.102
That's ridiculous.

34
00:01:49.507 --> 00:01:53.377
And I know it's being sold to
you that way, but it's a lie.

35
00:01:53.827 --> 00:01:57.937
See, there's a point where efficiency
just produces diminishing returns,

36
00:01:58.177 --> 00:02:02.437
especially in a field where quality is
all that differentiates your institution

37
00:02:02.437 --> 00:02:03.967
from the one down the street I.

38
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:09.547
We've been so distracted by the shiny AI
marketing, sales pitches, and addictive

39
00:02:09.547 --> 00:02:15.517
product design that we let a lot of
really bad, sometimes awful output slide.

40
00:02:16.057 --> 00:02:20.917
I hear more and more often how marketing
professionals are using chat, GPT, the

41
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:25.507
most popular LLM product, serving the
most general range of people trained on

42
00:02:25.507 --> 00:02:27.547
the most mainstream available content.

43
00:02:28.207 --> 00:02:30.007
And they're using it
to do writing for them.

44
00:02:30.907 --> 00:02:32.227
And I'm just gonna pause it.

45
00:02:32.227 --> 00:02:37.177
This question, if you take an objective
step back, is the quality of that

46
00:02:37.177 --> 00:02:39.337
output as good or better than content?

47
00:02:39.337 --> 00:02:40.957
You used to write the old fashioned way.

48
00:02:40.957 --> 00:02:42.832
I. Follow up question.

49
00:02:43.282 --> 00:02:46.672
Do you believe that a predictive text
model trained on the lowest common

50
00:02:46.672 --> 00:02:50.722
denominator of content is capable of
stringing together some words that will be

51
00:02:50.722 --> 00:02:52.882
effective in a highly competitive market?

52
00:02:53.812 --> 00:02:54.922
Here's the hard truth.

53
00:02:55.312 --> 00:02:57.832
Quality performs better than quantity.

54
00:02:58.372 --> 00:03:03.202
There's no point adding to the slop unless
you have no intention of standing out.

55
00:03:03.682 --> 00:03:05.272
AI generated ad imagery.

56
00:03:05.512 --> 00:03:08.572
It underperforms compared to
carefully design campaigns.

57
00:03:08.902 --> 00:03:13.522
AI generated content typically has
to be rewritten multiple times.

58
00:03:13.882 --> 00:03:18.052
AI built code will do not get me started.

59
00:03:18.582 --> 00:03:23.592
Creative work is inefficient and
inefficiency produces better quality.

60
00:03:24.102 --> 00:03:27.942
Doing more with less is never as
good as doing better with less.

61
00:03:28.572 --> 00:03:32.892
When efficiency is chased at all
costs, we end up misplacing quality.

62
00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:39.132
We end up promoting sloppy work producing
literal slop, and that hurts our brands

63
00:03:39.132 --> 00:03:43.272
and our students and our people in
competitive fields like higher ed.

64
00:03:43.272 --> 00:03:46.782
And I know I've said this
before, but our people are all

65
00:03:46.782 --> 00:03:48.552
we have that differentiates us.

66
00:03:48.927 --> 00:03:51.927
To compete, we have to
stop playing to the mean.

67
00:03:52.107 --> 00:03:55.677
And to do that, we have to reclaim
our core human characteristics.

68
00:03:56.247 --> 00:04:00.867
Quality comes from allocating time to
the right processes, Taking the time to

69
00:04:00.867 --> 00:04:05.427
listen process, consider, and then apply
what we know might be less efficient

70
00:04:05.427 --> 00:04:07.527
than asking an LLM to generate an idea.

71
00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:11.307
But nine times outta 10, it is going
to produce better, more original

72
00:04:11.307 --> 00:04:12.447
thought than that model will.

73
00:04:12.957 --> 00:04:15.507
Here's something else
to think about today.

74
00:04:15.507 --> 00:04:19.197
In 2025, the quote Doge model
of government overhaul is

75
00:04:19.197 --> 00:04:20.697
being bandied about globally.

76
00:04:21.297 --> 00:04:23.997
Ostensibly, this is about
increasing government efficiency,

77
00:04:24.237 --> 00:04:28.197
but it also ignores the fact that
government is inefficient by design.

78
00:04:28.437 --> 00:04:32.697
Democracy and public participation
in government have to be inefficient.

79
00:04:33.027 --> 00:04:38.097
Total efficiency means no participation,
no questioning, no auditing, no new

80
00:04:38.097 --> 00:04:40.497
ideas, no buy-in for the people.

81
00:04:41.202 --> 00:04:43.302
This is the core of my argument today.

82
00:04:43.572 --> 00:04:47.232
There's a point when the quest
for efficiency cancels out any

83
00:04:47.232 --> 00:04:49.122
sense of art or creativity.

84
00:04:49.482 --> 00:04:54.102
There's no thinking or considering or
challenging when we outsource to machines,

85
00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:56.172
the very spark that makes us human.

86
00:04:56.652 --> 00:04:59.262
So consider closely the things
that you choose to automate.

87
00:04:59.802 --> 00:05:03.522
Make sure you aren't passing the things
that need your unique take on them.

88
00:05:03.522 --> 00:05:04.542
Over to software.

89
00:05:04.902 --> 00:05:08.922
Don't fall into the trap of thinking
that just because an LLM can mimic

90
00:05:08.922 --> 00:05:11.862
your writing style, that it's
good enough for rock and roll.

91
00:05:12.282 --> 00:05:14.082
It's only low quality simulacra.

92
00:05:14.292 --> 00:05:19.002
It's soulless, lacking all aura, and
you deserve better than to have a cheap

93
00:05:19.002 --> 00:05:23.022
knockoff of your own brilliance out in
the world masquerading as the real thing.

94
00:05:23.618 --> 00:05:24.608
Have I convinced you yet?

95
00:05:24.968 --> 00:05:28.178
I know I probably haven't,
so let me try one more thing.

96
00:05:28.808 --> 00:05:32.378
We only have so many pieces
of our humanity to give away

97
00:05:32.378 --> 00:05:34.028
before we lose it entirely.

98
00:05:34.508 --> 00:05:38.858
What happens when, for efficiency's
sake, we forget how to write, how to

99
00:05:38.858 --> 00:05:41.678
code, how to design, how to tell stories.

100
00:05:42.053 --> 00:05:46.013
What happens when our core creative
spark is completely mediated by

101
00:05:46.043 --> 00:05:50.843
LLMs generative models and the huge
corporations serving their bottom lines?

102
00:05:51.653 --> 00:05:53.963
There's an argument
floating around the AI boom.

103
00:05:53.993 --> 00:05:56.843
Law isn't going to take people's jobs.

104
00:05:56.873 --> 00:05:58.223
It's just going to change them.

105
00:05:58.823 --> 00:05:59.843
And I agree.

106
00:06:00.203 --> 00:06:04.523
The AI boom makes us worse at our
jobs, makes our work more generic,

107
00:06:04.523 --> 00:06:07.763
and encourages us to forget the
things that make us unique in

108
00:06:07.763 --> 00:06:09.683
favor of conformity and hegemony.

109
00:06:10.553 --> 00:06:11.903
Quality is inefficient.

110
00:06:12.113 --> 00:06:13.433
Craft is inefficient.

111
00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:15.383
Brilliance is inefficient.

112
00:06:15.743 --> 00:06:17.633
Art is messy and inefficient.

113
00:06:17.993 --> 00:06:18.683
Let's face it.

114
00:06:18.683 --> 00:06:21.803
Humans are inefficient and
that's a beautiful thing.

115
00:06:23.003 --> 00:06:23.963
Thanks for listening.

116
00:06:23.993 --> 00:06:25.763
Connect with me Joel Goodman on Blue Sky.

